<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 source-aligned triangle and quadrilateral attributes"><h2>Attributes of Triangles and Quadrilaterals</h2>
<h3>Original four True/False expressions</h3><table class="source-chart"><tbody><tr><td>3×60=9×10</td><td>FALSE</td><td>180≠90</td></tr><tr><td>180=3×60</td><td>TRUE</td><td>180=180</td></tr><tr><td>6×40=24×10</td><td>TRUE</td><td>240=240</td></tr><tr><td>24×10=240</td><td>TRUE</td><td>240=240</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Activity1: Sort source Triangle Cards</h3><p>Teacher provides colored paper Triangle Cards to groups of2 or4. All shapes have3 sides, so classify by EQUAL SIDE LENGTHS, RIGHT ANGLE or other shared visible attributes; teacher checks actual card drawings. Original does not require new formal names equilateral/isosceles/scalene.</p>
<h3>Activity2: Sort TWENTY source Quadrilateral Cards</h3><p>Sort the separate original set of20 four-sided shape cards using equal side lengths, right-angle properties or edges that appear to run in the same direction. Students are NOT required to know the word parallel.</p>
<p>DISPLAY your sorted cards. Visit another group's display and guess its classification rule; then each group explains. Keep cards in bags/envelopes for next original lesson.</p>
<h3>Compare</h3><p>Discuss why a triangle can have at most one right angle but some quadrilaterals have several. Many defensible grouping choices exist; no publisher-specific card categories are fabricated.</p></section>